    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d440">

                <pb facs="0631" n="517" xml:id="pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.51/1499</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of a Conversation
                        with the Haitian Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dejean</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">on Special Mission
                        in the United States</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-11-02"><hi rendition="#italic">November 2,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Leon Dejean suggested that this was a very opportune moment to effect two
                    reforms in Haiti which he assured me would have a most excellent and farreaching
                    effect if they could be carried out.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>(1)</label>
                    <item>One was a combination into one office of the present two offices of the
                        Receiver General and the Financial Adviser. He spoke of the expense of
                        having these two high officials, and he thought that the administration of
                        the government would also be facilitated by having the work performed by one
                        official.</item>
                    <label>(2)</label>
                    <item>The other point which he raised was his desire to have civilians take
                        over, wherever possible, the work which was now being performed in the
                        administration by the military. He referred to the education of military
                        officers, which in all armies was the same. There was a desire to give
                        orders and to have them promptly obeyed. They did not have the same spirit
                        of cooperation which civilians would have by nature and by education. I
                        asked him whether he was disposed to put his suggestions into writing, and
                        he said that he preferred to bring the two points to our attention very
                        informally and orally.</item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed>W[<hi rendition="#small-caps">illiam</hi>] P[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">hillips</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
